[E0454] Error: `count` follows an implicit parameter
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:12:30 ]
    │
 12 │ fn early(implicit log: &Log, count: i64): i64 = count
    │          ─────────┬────────  ─────┬────
    │                   ╰────────────────────── the first implicit
    │                                   │
    │                                   ╰────── after an implicit
    │
    │ Note: implicit parameters come after the others, as a call that fills them writes the others alone
────╯
[E0454] Error: `level` is implicit and has a default
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:14:14 ]
    │
 14 │ fn defaulted(implicit level: i64 = 3): i64 = level
    │              ───────────┬───────────
    │                         ╰───────────── implicit here
    │
    │ Note: a call that passes nothing fills an implicit from its caller; a default would give a value nothing shows
────╯
[E0454] Error: `log` is implicit and owns what it holds
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:16:10 ]
    │
 16 │ fn owned(implicit log: Log): i64 = 0
    │          ────────┬────────
    │                  ╰────────── implicit here
    │
    │ Note: an implicit is lent, `&` or `&var`, or copied; a value that owns memory would be moved out of the caller at every call
────╯
[E0454] Error: `value` is implicit and may own what it holds
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:18:15 ]
    │
 18 │ fn generic<T>(implicit value: T): i64 = 0
    │               ────────┬────────
    │                       ╰────────── implicit here
    │
    │ Note: an implicit is lent, `&` or `&var`, or copied; a type parameter is copied only where it is `copy`, and may otherwise own memory, which would be moved out of the caller at every call
────╯
[E0454] Error: a C function takes no implicit parameter
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:21:17 ]
    │
 21 │     fn abs(n: i32, implicit log: &Log): i32
    │                    ─────────┬────────
    │                             ╰────────── implicit here
    │
    │ Note: C passes every argument it is given
────╯
[E0314] Error: `&Log` cannot be passed to or from C
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:21:31 ]
    │
 21 │     fn abs(n: i32, implicit log: &Log): i32
    │                                  ──┬─
    │                                    ╰─── not a C-compatible type
    │
    │ Note: extern functions can take and return only integers of up to 64 bits, `f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `cstring`, `ptr<T>` and C's structs; a parameter may also be a `&T`, or an `Option<&T>`, which pass as a pointer, null for `.None`, a `str` or a `&[T]`, which pass as a pointer and a length, an array by reference, which passes as a pointer to its first element, or a function that takes and answers no struct by value, and no function that may be null, and whose own types cross, which passes as a C function pointer; a result may be `never`, for a C function that does not return
────╯
[E0341] Error: `write` does not match `Report`
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:33:5 ]
    │
 27 │     fn write(implicit log: &Log): i64
    │        ──┬──
    │          ╰──── declared here
    │
 33 │     fn write(log: &Log): i64 = 0
    │        ──┬──
    │          ╰──── `log` is not implicit, and the interface's is
    │
    │ Note: a call through the interface fills the implicits it declares, by their names, so an implementation declares them as it does
────╯
[E0456] Error: `logged` takes implicit parameters, so it cannot be a value
    ╭─[ err/471_implicit_declared/main.wip:37:10 ]
    │
 24 │ fn logged(implicit log: &Log): i64 = 1
    │ ─────────────────┬────────────────
    │                  ╰────────────────── declared here
    │
 37 │     val f = logged
    │             ───┬──
    │                ╰──── taken as a value here
    │
    │ Help: write a lambda that calls it, `(x) => logged(x)`, which fills them where it is written
